---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../lib/site';
---

<Layout title="Contribute — Open Allergy Cards">
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Contribute</h2>
    <p style="max-width: 46rem;">
      Every card on this site is generated from plain text files in
      <a href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">our GitHub repository</a>.
      The most valuable contribution is <strong>translation review by native speakers</strong> —
      it takes five minutes and no coding.
    </p>

    <h3>Verify a translation (no coding required)</h3>
    <ol class="steps" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
      <li>
        <h3>Find the file</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/data/allergens`}>data/allergens/</a> on GitHub and
          pick an allergen, e.g. <code>milk.yaml</code>. Each language has its own section.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Review the wording</h3>
        <p>
          Click the pencil icon to edit in your browser. Fix anything that doesn't read naturally —
          this text is shown to restaurant staff, so clarity beats literal translation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Mark it verified</h3>
        <p>
          If the translation is accurate, change <code>verified: false</code> to
          <code>verified: true</code> and open a pull request mentioning that you're a native or
          fluent speaker.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Add a language or allergen</h3>
    <p style="max-width: 46rem;">
      New languages go in <code>data/languages.yaml</code>; new allergens are a single new YAML
      file copied from an existing one. Full instructions are in
      <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a>. Every pull request
      is automatically checked against the data schema, so you can't break the site.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Why your help matters</h3>
    <p style="max-width: 46rem; color: var(--ink-soft);">
      A mistranslated allergy card is a safety risk. Machine translation gets us a first draft;
      only a native speaker can confirm it says exactly the right thing in a real restaurant.
      Cards with unverified translations show a warning until someone like you signs off.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
